409→                    
410→                           Thue proved that the minimal such diameter is achieved (asymptotically) by the points in a triangular lattice intersected with a circle. In general Erdős believed such a set must have very large intersection with the triangular lattice (perhaps as many as $(1-o(1))n$).<br><br>Erdős <a href="#cite-Er94b-99" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er94b','99'); return false;">[Er94b]</a> wrote 'I could not prove it but felt that it should not be hard. To my great surprise both B. H. Sendov and M. Simonovits doubted the truth of this conjecture.' In <a href="#cite-Er94b-99" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er94b','99'); return false;">[Er94b]</a> he offers \$100 for a counterexample but only \$50 for a proof.<br><br>The stated problem is false for $n=4$, for example taking the points to be vertices of a square. The behaviour of such sets for small $n$ is explored by Bezdek and Fodor <a href="#cite-BeFo99-99" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('BeFo99','99'); return false;">[BeFo99]</a>.<br><br>See also <a href="/103" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">[103]</a>.
